    {
      "heading": "Intro",
      "beats": [
        {
          "say": "Every second, the debt of the United States government grows by about eighty thousand dollars.",
          "show": {
            "type": "number",
            "big": "$80,000",
            "caption": "added to the US debt every second, fiscal 2026",
            "color": "red"
          }
        },
        {
          "say": "In the fiscal year that just ended, it grew by two and a half trillion dollars. And on August eighteenth, it passed forty trillion for the first time in history.",
          "show": {
            "type": "number",
            "big": "$37.6T → $40.2T",
            "caption": "US debt, Sept 2025 → Sept 2026",
            "color": "red"
          }
        },
        {
          "say": "Split across everyone who lives in the country, that's about a hundred and eighteen thousand dollars per person.",
          "show": {
            "type": "number",
            "big": "$118,000",
            "caption": "US debt per person, October 2026",
            "color": "red"
          }
        },
        {
          "say": "But the size isn't the most surprising part. The surprising part is the bill that comes with it."
        },
        {
          "say": "Because this year, for the first time, the interest alone has passed a trillion dollars. More than the entire military. Let's see how that happened – with the official numbers.",
          "show": {
            "type": "note",
            "text": "Interest on the debt now costs *more than the military.*",
            "source": "Monthly Treasury Statement, fiscal year 2026 through August",
            "color": "red"
          }
        }
