    {
      "heading": "What the debt is",
      "beats": [
        {
          "say": "First, what is the national debt? It's simply everything the federal government has borrowed and not yet paid back.",
          "show": {
            "type": "note",
            "text": "*Deficit* = one year of spending more than you take in. *Debt* = all the deficits added up.",
            "source": "US Treasury, Fiscal Data"
          }
        },
        {
          "say": "The government borrows by selling bonds – Treasury bills, notes and bonds. People, banks, pension funds, foreign governments and the Federal Reserve buy them, and get paid interest."
        },
        {
          "say": "Why does it borrow? Because it spends more than it collects. In fiscal 2025, the government took in about five point two trillion dollars and spent about seven trillion.",
          "show": {
            "type": "compare",
            "heading": "US federal budget, fiscal 2025",
            "left": [
              "taken in",
              "$5.24T"
            ],
            "right": [
              "spent",
              "$7.01T"
            ],
            "color": "red"
          }
        },
        {
          "say": "That gap – about one point eight trillion dollars – is the deficit. It gets borrowed, and it lands on top of the debt."
        },
        {
          "say": "Put another way: for every dollar that came in, about a dollar thirty-four went out.",
          "show": {
            "type": "compare",
            "heading": "For every dollar in…",
            "left": [
              "came in",
              "$1.00"
            ],
            "right": [
              "went out",
              "$1.34"
            ],
            "color": "red"
          }
        }
