    {
      "heading": "Where the borrowing comes from",
      "beats": [
        {
          "say": "So why does the gap never close? The last time the federal budget ran a surplus was in 2001. Every year since, it has borrowed.",
          "show": {
            "type": "number",
            "big": "2001",
            "caption": "the last year with a federal budget surplus",
            "color": "gold"
          }
        },
        {
          "say": "Part of it is shocks. When a recession hits, tax revenue drops and spending on support rises – automatically. The deficit was one point four trillion in 2009, and three point one trillion in 2020.",
          "show": {
            "type": "compare",
            "heading": "Deficits in crisis years",
            "left": [
              "2009",
              "$1.41T"
            ],
            "right": [
              "2020",
              "$3.10T"
            ],
            "color": "red"
          }
        },
        {
          "say": "Part of it is structure. The two biggest programs, Social Security and Medicare, grow as more people retire. In fiscal 2025 they cost about two point six trillion dollars together."
        },
        {
          "say": "And who is owed the money? Most of it – about thirty-two trillion – is held by the public: investors, banks, funds, foreign governments, the Federal Reserve.",
          "show": {
            "type": "compare",
            "heading": "Who holds the debt, Sept 2026",
